  "summary": "New tools address storage, regression throughput, data access and reproducible reporting as cohort-scale omics data outgrow existing formats and workflows.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is infrastructure that makes established analyses tractable at cohort or genome scale, rather than new analytical paradigms. pbzarr stores per-base data for thousands of samples in Zarr and is reported to be faster and smaller than D4 and bigWig. RapidGLM, a C++ library, is reported to accelerate count-regression differential expression, CRISPR screen and eQTL analyses 2.5-17.8x. A second thread is accessible, standardised interfaces: pyGENIE gives unified API access to AACR GENIE with TMB and mutational signature integration, and Met4All packages methylation array QC, normalisation, differential methylation and copy-number analysis in a dockerised Shiny app. CATEomics contributes model selection and ensembling for heterogeneous treatment effect estimation on high-dimensional omics data using proxy losses on out-of-fold estimates.",
